A||N||18||BellaFiero||Stereo Equipment and Vendors||||4||
Z||000000||BellaFiero||08-03-2000||10:14 AM||||So I'm out cruising with my fiance in my Bella Donna, and there's a body-kit-fitted Civic four-door at the red light. I pull up next to him, and his system is making MY system shake! So I wave him over to the parking lot of Audio Express. We get out, and I ask him what's up - how did he get so much bass crammed in there? So he shows me his set-up - Stealth boxes. Very nice looking. He's got speakers on the roof of his car, facing down, and under his seats, facing to the front. Under the dash, pointing down, and two subs in the trunk, facing the driver and passenger. The sales guy from Audio Express comes out and starts bragging that he did the Civic, and the Civic-driver ( Aaron ) nods. SO then he starts tearing up my car, literally. He criticizes me for my placement of the 10" in the footwell, for my choice of head unit, my speakers... and then starts quoting prices to me. He says it will cost $200 for a custom sub box to go in the footwell, $300 for a box to go up front under the hood, $250 for custom speaker boxes that will form-fit the area behind the headrests and on the  interior side of the sail panels ( to hold 6 x 9s ). <BR>SO - here's my questions folks...<BR>A) Formula, will you make that custom box for the front for me? The prices the vendors around here want are SICK. <BR>B) How can I get 6 x 9s in my baby? Either up front or mounted somewhere custom?<BR>C) IS there any way to increase the size of the rear panel speakers? I understand the mesh cover might not fit anymore - just what can I do? Has anyone tried this yet?<BR>D) The center glove box... I've seen pics of it somehow containing a large 8" or 10". I'm installing an overhead compartment this weekend to hold magazines, papers, etc. with switches to also control my foglights, my tracers, and my neon kits, etc. Has anyone ever seen this mod up close ( speaker in the center console glovebox ). <BR>E) The front map pocket, in front of the passenger - is there any space behind there? Can you cut into it without hurting anything? <P>Thanks for any replies, guys... I think I'm going to be pretty busy this weekend with just preliminary looks and ideas... any help you give me, any ideas or past experiences, I'd appreciate it!!<P>Jayse<P>------------------<BR>"No, no... you can't put that there, it'll be in the way of the Johnson Rod!"  ||||reg||4
Z||000001||b lo 0||08-03-2000||10:58 AM||||As far as speakers go, DON'T go with 6x9s.  Sure, they sound great, but you can make it sound better.  Get a 4" component set, mount the 4" mid in the 4x9 location, and put the tweeter at the top of the A-pillar.  For the rear, either don't worry about it (some competition systems don't even have rear fill speakers) or put some 6.5" components in a box mounted slightly below head height.  As for the subs, tell the guy to shove it.  Where you have it is fine.  Take it from me, I've been installing for several years now.  (plus I'm not out to cheat you out of your money)<P>------------------<BR>'85 GT (in progress)  <BR>titled ICE-plates B LO 0 <BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.angelfire.com/ga3/audioimagery/ice.gif">    <BR>Go to <A HREF="http://www.audioimagery.i-p.com" TARGET=_blank>www.audioimagery.i-p.com</A>  !!!||||reg||1
Z||000002||Monkeyman||08-03-2000||11:01 AM||||Forenote:  I am not an audio expert, but I've listened well to others in the past.<P>A)  Formula--I want one, too.<P>B)  I don't think you would want 6x9s.  Sound waves are round, therefore it would make more sense to go with a round speaker.  Besides, 6x9s are so '80s.<P>C)  Yes and no.  The only way Pontiac could make the 4x6s fit in the sail panels was to cut part of the metal behind the plastic inner panel.  I guess you could cut out more of the metal and fab your own brackets, but why?<P>D)  batboy has something like what you're talking about.  Try looking for a pic of it on his site.<BR>Here is a pic of the commercial version.<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/speaker_2.jpg"> <BR>I think it's made by RSM or IRM.<P>E)  Absolutely not.  The back of the "glovebox"  just about rests against the heater/AC ducts.  You'd be hard pressed just to get your hand back there.<P>In speaking with lots of audiophiles (some from right here on the forum), the best way I can see to get a great sound (without draining your wallet) would be to go with a set of good separates.  WTIII has 6 1/2"ers mounted on the floor with the tweeters mounted in the lower outside vents.  I'll either do it that way (if I can figure out a way to make some nice enclosures) or mount 5 1/4"ers in the dash using a plate.  That way the sound should bounce off of the windshield.  I'm not convinced that you really need a rear fill.  I've listened to WTIIIs system (no rear fill) and it sounds <B>good</B>.  WTIII has his sub mounted in the passenger footwell, but without an enclosure (yet).  If you want rear fill, you might try a small (?4"?) speaker mounted in your glovebox/ECM cover.  ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Philphine||08-03-2000||11:06 AM||||do some searching around. i came across a couple of pairs of infinity reference 8" 3 ways that i'm planing to put in the dead pedal area of a couple of fieros. at this point i am still working out how but it definitly looks possible. if you have power windows and no map pockets consider illusion audio 8's in each door. i may even try some free air tens. i like subs in front and in the passenger compartment.<P>guess thats all experimental. do a search for tried and true ideas. there are at least a couple of people with award winning systems.  <P>------------------<BR>Phil T<P>'87 coupe (feral)<BR>'84 indy<BR>'84 coupe||||reg||1
